{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the staged branch ledger on node1 while retaining the original ledger IDs and amounts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 contains the staged rows 1001/12.50 and 1002/-3.25, and the final state retains them unchanged alongside the replication test rows.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f0699522a6944d55","cmd-d789f341d5b44e54","cmd-e3da44128e02413c"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Establish node2 as a read-only application replica, with node1 remaining the application writer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final configuration shows node1 writable and node2 read-only. A non-administrative account granted INSERT was directly rejected on node2, and both nodes finished with matching ledgers and healthy services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7c3971697cc4ad0","cmd-a33a8d47df714d5a","cmd-e3da44128e02413c"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Use a replication-only account.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Replication uses ledger_repl, restricted to node2's address. Final privilege inspection shows only REPLICATION SLAVE and zero schema or table privileges.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c7c999b813904e7d","cmd-e3da44128e02413c"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Committed inserts on node1 arrive on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After row 1003 was committed on node1, node2 directly showed that row with the same amount, matching executed/read positions, both replication threads running, and zero lag.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ecf4c17605a641a4","cmd-c6ec16186e8049fb"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"After a short replica disconnect, node2 catches up without losing or duplicating ledger rows.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The captured transition shows a healthy three-row baseline, both replica threads stopped, two committed primary writes while node2 remained behind, and successful recovery to five rows with five distinct IDs, the correct 39.50 total, matching position 1642, zero lag, and no replication errors.","evidence_ids":["cmd-077f8fd1aa0240c7","cmd-30fbc0d541e24443","cmd-6e29cb0d9d324d28","cmd-1dca82e8589a4268"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Preserve the staged export in /srv/legacy2/mysql-replication-catchup.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node1 export remained at the required path with its original 196-byte size, permissions, and SHA-256 hash matching the preparation baseline.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-f0699522a6944d55","cmd-a8cf4c89b4004cdc","cmd-e3da44128e02413c"]},{"id":"R7","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"MariaDB 11.4.12 was installed from the Alpine 3.23 repositories. Final observations show both nodes still running Alpine 3.23.3 with kernel 6.18.7-0-lts, unchanged from the pre-executor platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6d62a95ea462466b","cmd-b098ac6612e34f4c","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.76,"summary":"The implementation was functional and package health remained unchanged, but several avoidable mutations or residues were present. The failed root-SCP approach installed root SSH authorization and private-key material on node1 and was not shown cleaned up (cmd-d0f38ce820e64cc0). The node2 transfer setup also installed an unnecessary inbound root authorized key, while its outbound private key was needed for the successful transfer; neither was shown removed (cmd-a6943d31f3f34c40). Replication secrets created or copied as bootstrap files were retained without a demonstrated ongoing need (cmd-d789f341d5b44e54, cmd-cbd32d5ba7c44070). The temporary transfer account and staging were relevant and were subsequently removed, so they are not penalized as abandoned state (cmd-0ebf19a04d4748b2, cmd-a8cf4c89b4004cdc). The bootstrap SQL remained in /tmp on node2, and restart log files remained on both nodes (cmd-cbd32d5ba7c44070, cmd-9189554066f44931, cmd-d4ca7996236c4216). MariaDB listening on port 3306 is task-related, and no new package inconsistency or failed task service was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0f38ce820e64cc0","cmd-a6943d31f3f34c40","cmd-d789f341d5b44e54","cmd-cbd32d5ba7c44070","cmd-0ebf19a04d4748b2","cmd-a8cf4c89b4004cdc","cmd-9189554066f44931","cmd-d4ca7996236c4216","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Replication was demonstrated both during normal operation and through a bounded disconnect-and-catch-up transition. The mandatory restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and connection loss, changed boot identities independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, and replication state.","limitations":["The lifecycle failed-service probe was unsupported because it checks systemd on Alpine; service health is instead supported by direct OpenRC and MariaDB checks.","Final lifecycle snapshots did not inventory /root, so retained SSH and secret files are inferred from their creation and the absence of any captured cleanup rather than independently confirmed by the final snapshot.","No explicit post-task hash was captured for node2's original export path; preservation is directly proven for node1, while node2 bootstrap used a separate /tmp copy."]}
